In TypeScript 2.0: (a) Immediately after x = 1, control flow type analysis
narrows the type of x from string | number to number (specifically the numeric
literal type 1). (b) Accessing x.length produces a compile-time type error
because property length does not exist on type number. (c) A minimal correction
captures the length before reassignment or avoids reassigning x:

if (typeof x === 'string') {
  const len = x.length;
  x = 1;
  return len;
}

Alternatively, simply return x.length directly without modifying x.

In Node.js 12.0.0: (a) No, cb2 is not guaranteed to receive 'new'. (b)
Calling fs.writeFile followed by fs.readFile simply schedules two asynchronous
I/O tasks in libuv's thread pool. Because disk operations run
non-deterministically, readFile may read the file before writeFile finishes
writing it. (c) Nest the read inside the write callback with error checking:

fs.writeFile('x.txt', 'new', (err) => {
  if (err) throw err;
  fs.readFile('x.txt', 'utf8', (err, data) => {
    if (err) throw err;
    cb2(null, data);
  });
});

In TypeScript 2.0 with strictNullChecks: (a) The non-null assertion
operator ! tells the compiler to remove null and undefined from the type of the
operand, suppressing compile-time nullability errors. (b) It adds no runtime
guard whatsoever because it is completely erased during compilation; passing
undefined causes a runtime TypeError: Cannot read property 'name' of undefined.
(c) A safe runtime guard:

function readName(user: { name: string } | undefined) {
  if (user !== undefined) {
    return user.name;
  }
}
